---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import RadioGroupDemo from "../../../components/RadioGroupDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";

const example = `import { RadioGroup, RadioGroupItem } from "@/components/ui/radio-group";

<RadioGroup
  legend="Delivery"
  name="delivery"
  value={delivery}
  onValueChange={setDelivery}
>
  <RadioGroupItem value="standard">Standard</RadioGroupItem>
  <RadioGroupItem value="express">Express</RadioGroupItem>
  <RadioGroupItem value="drone" disabled>
    Drone (unavailable)
  </RadioGroupItem>
</RadioGroup>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="radio-group">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    The browser provides the keyboard model, so there is no focus code to get wrong.
  </Fragment>

  <RadioGroupDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>Named by its legend.</strong> <code>RadioGroup</code> is a
        <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code>, and <code>legend</code> is required: it is the visible
        question and the group's accessible name.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One Tab stop, arrow keys to choose.</strong> The radios share one <code>name</code>
        (generated if you do not pass one), so the browser makes the group one Tab stop, on the
        checked radio or the first one. The arrow keys move focus and the choice, and skip disabled
        radios.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Controlled or not.</strong> Pass <code>value</code> and update it from
        <code>onValueChange</code>, or pass <code>defaultValue</code>. Forms submit the
        <code>name</code> with the checked <code>value</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Labels are click targets.</strong> Each item is a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> row at
        least 24px tall, so clicking the text checks the radio. <code>className</code> styles that
        row; other props go to the <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Errors.</strong> ARIA allows <code>aria-invalid</code> on a radio but not on a
        group, so pass <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> to each item; its border turns destructive.
        Point the group's <code>aria-describedby</code> at the error text, so it is read with the
        legend.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>States.</strong> A checked radio shows a filled dot, not only a color. Focus shows
        the ring outline. <code>disabled</code> on the group disables every radio; on an item, only
        that one. In forced-colors mode the native radio returns, drawn by the system.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="examples">
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Controlled, with a disabled option</h3>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={example} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/radio-group.tsx</code>, the same path as shadcn's Radio Group.
    Unlike shadcn's, it renders a fieldset with a required <code>legend</code>, and each item takes
    its label as children. It needs only your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your
    <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing
    it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
